.TH CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER \- declare buffer for ranged memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER(
    name,
    smallest_size_power_of_two,
    largest_size_power_of_two,
    number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a uint8_t array with the correct size for a ranged memory pool
that has the given parameters. The caller can then give the buffer to
ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). The macro includes the
status byte of every element of every sub-pool in the size that it
computes. The buffer holds every sub-pool in contiguous memory.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name                                     Variable name for the buffer
smallest_size_power_of_two               Exponent for smallest pool size
largest_size_power_of_two                Exponent for largest pool size
number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two  Exponent for smallest pool count
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The buffer goes on the stack or into static storage, by context
This is useful for embedded systems, and when you do not want a heap
  allocation
The macro declares the buffer with the alignment that every entry the
  pool hands out is sure to meet. That alignment is 16 bytes. You can
  therefore give the buffer directly to
  ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). You do not have to
  think about alignment. The result is that every single sub-pool
  segment inside the buffer stays correctly aligned. The 16 is a fixed
  constant. CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(3) gives the
  reason
The macro sizes the buffer exactly as
  ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() requires for the same
  parameters
A _Static_assert rejects three cases at compile time. The first is a set
  of parameters that would make the required buffer size overflow
  size_t. The second is a number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two
  below largest_size_power_of_two - smallest_size_power_of_two. The
  third is a smallest_size_power_of_two below 4. The smallest element
  that a ranged pool serves is 16 bytes, and a buffer declared for
  anything smaller could only go to a constructor that refuses it.
  Without the assert, the macro silently produces an array of the wrong
  size
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER(my_buffer, 4, 8, 10);
ccol_r_mempool *rmp = ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    my_buffer, sizeof(my_buffer), 4, 8, 10,
    ccol_fallback_disabled, false, NULL, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer (3)
